{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":14025633,"ns":0,"title":"Bipolar transistor biasing","extract":"Biasing is the setting of the DC operating point of an electronic component. For bipolar junction transistors (BJTs), the operating point is defined as the steady-state DC collector-emitter voltage (\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          V\n          \n            \n              c\n              e\n            \n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle V_{\\mathrm {ce} }}\n  \n) and the collector current (\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          I\n          \n            \n              c\n            \n          \n        \n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle I_{\\mathrm {c} }}\n  \n) with no input signal applied.","description":"Process necessary for BJT amplifiers to work correctly","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}